                <pb facs="0314" n="306" sameAs="frus:frus1938v02/pg306"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.248/68: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-05-17T14:00:00+01:00">May 17, 1938—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 17—8:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>778. Before I left Washington Mr. Joseph Green showed me a reply prepared by his
                    Division to a request from the French Government for a definition of our policy
                    with regard to the delivery of airplanes to France in case of war between France
                    and Germany. I should be greatly obliged if a copy of this reply could be
                    forwarded to me by pouch immediately.</p>
                <p>I was astonished a few days ago to find that the Minister of Air La Chambre had
                    been left with the impression by Baron de La Grange that deliveries of American
                    planes to France would be continued even though France and Germany might be at
                    war. I did my utmost to convince the Minister that he should not count on
                    receiving a single American plane after a declaration of war; but feel that I
                    should reinforce my verbal statements by handing him a written communication
                    giving the point of view of our Government on this subject.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
